1e3c015adad2c826a64e255bd982941dc9fe50679acc2bae3f00b31bd9f39.png)" 13194139535866 true] [17592186105692 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371181 13194139535866 true] [17592186105692 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371237 13194139535866 true] [17592186105692 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371244 13194139535866 true] [17592186105693 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371216 13194139535866 true] [17592186105693 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371230 13194139535866 true] [17592186105693 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371258 13194139535866 true] [17592186105693 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371265 13194139535866 true] [17592186105694 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371202 13194139535866 true] [17592186105694 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371209 13194139535866 true] [17592186105694 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371223 13194139535866 true] [17592186105694 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371251 13194139535866 true] [17592186105711 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371188 13194139535866 true] [17592186105711 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371195 13194139535866 true] [17592186105711 1020 #edb/ref 17592186371272 13194139535866 true] [17592186106228 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535866 true] [17592186106228 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/190af2ef92a8c23efb6fc15c6effebf5db929d8d133da6809bd27f77a6e9292b.png)\n\nThe vector $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $-1.$ Find the vector $Av.$" 13194139535866 true] [17592186106228 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1375/Source/Images/q-37091.png)\n\nThe vector $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $-1$. Find the vector $Av$." 13194139535866 false] [17592186106228 1145 "The eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =-1v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/1f778e38d2dd4d2e33675bfa45bbe13c9596b1818092ef6129e8f35b1741157e.png)" 13194139535866 true] [17592186333663 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/61ed37aa6a5e67cdc60cca2c93969a47d12e1e36f18edf96f646922877641e79.png)\n\nThe vector $u$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{2}.$ Find the vector $Au.$" 13194139535866 false] [17592186333663 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/61ed37aa6a5e67cdc60cca2c93969a47d12e1e36f18edf96f646922877641e79.png)\n\nThe vector $u$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{2}.$ Find the vector $Au.$" 13194139535866 true] [17592186333663 1145 "The eigenvector $u=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au & =\\frac{1}{2}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14056271/assets/c272ff1454ac4ab9825e312f563c9bca08ac2800faa0e95ab45e8a5e827369da.png)" 13194139535866 false] [17592186333663 1145 "The eigenvector $u=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Au & =\\frac{1}{2}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/c272ff1454ac4ab9825e312f563c9bca08ac2800faa0e95ab45e8a5e827369da.png)" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371181 1017 #edb/ref 17592186371182 13194139535866 true] [17592186371181 1089 "q-106775" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371181 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535866 true] [17592186371181 1100 "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371181 1113 #uuid "56a4f3a9-ccca-53d2-8026-09ec5330fdbb" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371181 1145 "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $v$ is an eigenvector, then there must exist a real number $\\lambda $ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ Hence, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]=\\lambda [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis leads to the following system of equations for $\\lambda :$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}16=2\\lambda  \\\\ -8=-4\\lambda \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has no solution. Therefore, $v$ is not an eigenvector of $A.$" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371182 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371183 13194139535866 true] [17592186371182 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371184 13194139535866 true] [17592186371182 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371185 13194139535866 true] [17592186371182 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371186 13194139535866 true] [17592186371182 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371187 13194139535866 true] [17592186371182 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535866 true] [17592186371182 1041 "selection" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371182 1104 #edb/ref 17592186371184 13194139535866 true] [17592186371182 1110 #uuid "12f19e8f-2221-5360-bf0d-5edb74f3630d" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371183 1034 "-8" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371183 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535866 true] [17592186371183 1111 #uuid "0aeb963b-fbfa-56f5-89f9-e052c82bed07" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371184 1034 "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371184 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535866 true] [17592186371184 1111 #uuid "4b15870c-a2de-50b5-8309-f8086e971e65" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371185 1034 "8" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371185 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535866 true] [17592186371185 1111 #uuid "b2a446d5-95ea-5fde-84d1-ee7b75b562c5" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371186 1034 "2" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371186 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535866 true] [17592186371186 1111 #uuid "90bcdddc-cfaf-56fd-ba01-40fdf4797d42" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371187 1034 "-4" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371187 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535866 true] [17592186371187 1111 #uuid "6a124d98-9f14-5eeb-9563-c3b4fae1d486" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371188 1017 #edb/ref 17592186371189 13194139535866 true] [17592186371188 1089 "q-106890" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371188 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535866 true] [17592186371188 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/4adc01d460f42f134f2e6c8da1e2fe516c8e6cfb64bcfeb7a5db89cf93f1fc11.png)\n\nThe vector $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $C$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $-4.$ Find the vector $Cv.$" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371188 1113 #uuid "8bb61574-69ec-5f86-9b0c-3dbbf6ec3612" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371188 1145 "The eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-4.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Cv & =-4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/99e3776bfa4048703042970a2479a86c749e3e2a547dce966fceb23b671d66f2.png)" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371189 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371190 13194139535866 true] [17592186371189 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371191 13194139535866 true] [17592186371189 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371192 13194139535866 true] [17592186371189 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371193 13194139535866 true] [17592186371189 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371194 13194139535866 true] [17592186371189 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 1319413953586