198 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/9ad126d502b24d3195cbe56948819119fde18adc217efd95d6b74c623958f8be.png" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371198 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535866 true] [17592186371198 1111 #uuid "8c6f939f-3bd3-5a58-9935-dfa3145ce793" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371199 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/2e9b0004ae889fb63f5ae65bd2a79915b6e195935fd3b610d2f9957d10f6a9e7.png" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371199 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535866 true] [17592186371199 1111 #uuid "b0c2a2d7-46ee-57da-bdd0-acaafe70b70b" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371200 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/234cbbc4a4179a09e4bf56f1c428b2d0135961d381f800bb0c87787c444a1291.png" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371200 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535866 true] [17592186371200 1111 #uuid "8b146ea0-b85d-5a62-bc28-7aa8570e314f" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371201 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14071103/assets/74419ef803ba73230d983d96fa36ff323a0a0a95f9f400f2409a4b29cbd40ac6.png" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371201 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045452 13194139535866 true] [17592186371201 1111 #uuid "65158900-3a5f-556b-9cca-d1a5e278f7c7" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371202 1017 #edb/ref 17592186371203 13194139535866 true] [17592186371202 1089 "q-106902" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371202 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535866 true] [17592186371202 1100 "The eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}k & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2k\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =k+2$ is  $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ k-1\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k.$" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371202 1113 #uuid "4a90b8ce-e812-571d-b6d3-16961f18158d" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371202 1145 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}k & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ k-1\\end{aligned}] & =(k+2)[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ k-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2k+1 \\\\ -2+2{k}^{2}-2k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-k-2 \\\\ {k}^{2}+k-2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2k+1=-k-2, \\\\ -2+2{k}^{2}-2k={k}^{2}+k-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-k=-3\\,⟹\\,k=3.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $k=3$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2+2{k}^{2}-2k & ={k}^{2}+k-2 \\\\ -2+2\\cdot (3)^{2}-2\\cdot 3 & =(3)^{2}+3-2 \\\\ 10 & =10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=3.$" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371203 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371204 13194139535866 true] [17592186371203 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371205 13194139535866 true] [17592186371203 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371206 13194139535866 true] [17592186371203 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371207 13194139535866 true] [17592186371203 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371208 13194139535866 true] [17592186371203 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535866 true] [17592186371203 1041 "selection" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371203 1104 #edb/ref 17592186371205 13194139535866 true] [17592186371203 1110 #uuid "bb60fbee-6593-5091-928c-930fcf23a5f9" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371204 1034 "10" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371204 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535866 true] [17592186371204 1111 #uuid "4a1cf072-f5b2-5678-9872-81e48397fd3c" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371205 1034 "3" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371205 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535866 true] [17592186371205 1111 #uuid "9df924aa-9f87-522a-9655-5f3f3e055ed0" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371206 1034 "2" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371206 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535866 true] [17592186371206 1111 #uuid "87cf909f-12d3-5fc0-b634-fafe0460db32" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371207 1034 "1" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371207 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535866 true] [17592186371207 1111 #uuid "0e8ff9be-1c48-5726-aaaa-4af5ed8d9270" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371208 1034 "0" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371208 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535866 true] [17592186371208 1111 #uuid "4181df71-f3a3-5c22-b1a3-914f4af3f8bf" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371209 1017 #edb/ref 17592186371210 13194139535866 true] [17592186371209 1089 "q-106945" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371209 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535866 true] [17592186371209 1100 "An eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to an eigenvalue $\\lambda =6$ is  $[\\begin{aligned}c+32 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $c.$" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371209 1113 #uuid "72278b28-d35d-5d52-918b-adacb550b39d" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371209 1145 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}c+32 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] & =6[\\begin{aligned}c+32 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4c+236 \\\\ 2c+28\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6c+192 \\\\ 72\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4c+236=6c+192 \\\\ 2c+28=72\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n2c+28=72\\,⟹\\,c=22.\n$$\n\n- Solving for $c$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n4c+236=6c+192\\,⟹\\,c=22.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $c=22.$" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371210 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371211 13194139535866 true] [17592186371210 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371212 13194139535866 true] [17592186371210 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371213 13194139535866 true] [17592186371210 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371214 13194139535866 true] [17592186371210 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371215 13194139535866 true] [17592186371210 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535866 true] [17592186371210 1041 "selection" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371210 1104 #edb/ref 17592186371213 13194139535866 true] [17592186371210 1110 #uuid "1ad6613d-e948-568a-b7f5-0eabe299c80e" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371211 1034 "12" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371211 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535866 true] [17592186371211 1111 #uuid "ebea0d86-1323-5fe5-8869-3a11a6d61a40" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371212 1034 "20" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371212 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535866 true] [17592186371212 1111 #uuid "b87db648-1d01-51d2-adb6-b325ed28cbb9" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371213 1034 "22" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371213 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535866 true] [17592186371213 1111 #uuid "9b1ced1c-2e75-52c2-919e-c17afd3a8559" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371214 1034 "26" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371214 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535866 true] [17592186371214 1111 #uuid "a061c4ae-2b3b-5920-a222-a2cedffb9155" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371215 1034 "6" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371215 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535866 true] [17592186371215 1111 #uuid "4b73500b-bfeb-5121-83dc-2bacc3649339" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371216 1017 #edb/ref 17592186371217 13194139535866 true] [17592186371216 1089 "q-107314" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371216 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535866 true] [17592186371216 1100 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{7}$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =7.$\n- An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =1.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2},$ then $-v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371216 1113 #uuid "43d63a39-99ac-5de8-969c-b2b73de1a528" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371216 1145 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=\\frac{1}{7}v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{7}{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $7$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=7v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $7.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{-1}=-1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(-v)=-Av=-(\\frac{1}{2}v)=\\frac{1}{2}(-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(-v)=\\frac{1}{2}(-v),$ and $-v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $-v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." 13194139535866 true] [17592186371217 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371218 13194139535866 true] [17592186371217 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371219 13194139535866 true] [17592186371217 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371220 13194139535866 true] [17592186371217 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371221 13194139535866 true] [17592186371217 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371222 13194139535866 true] [17592186371217 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535866 true] [17592186371217 1041 "selection" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371217 1104 #edb/ref 17592186371219 13194139535866 true] [17592186371217 1110 #uuid "b4843aca-9f17-515a-989d-db53723de0df" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371218 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371218 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535866 true] [17592186371218 1111 #uuid "dec01ba0-aeec-5fcb-beae-7176e0184b80" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371219 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371219 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535866 true] [17592186371219 1111 #uuid "d8423897-772a-5ab5-a0b6-f859ce44de59" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371220 1034 "III only" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371220 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535866 true] [17592186371220 1111 #uuid "4f5fa2bf-f429-5b6a-9c23-83970c257a40" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371221 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371221 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535866 true] [17592186371221 1111 #uuid "2cee80e0-95a5-5db9-ad0a-e0bb7c198b70" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371222 1034 "II only" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371222 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535866 true] [17592186371222 1111 #uuid "e4a5a49c-1d87-5a55-b8a5-ab4db05c8306" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371223 1017 #edb/ref 17592186371224 13194139535866 true] [17592186371223 1089 "q-107842" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371223 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535866 true] [17592186371223 1100 "The eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -8+k \\\\ 3 & 5k\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2k$ is  $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k.$" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371223 1113 #uuid "6c8c1613-f0c1-51af-80b3-8266221c5212" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371223 1145 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -8+k \\\\ 3 & 5k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =2k[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}k-10 \\\\ -6+5k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-4k \\\\ 2k\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}k-10=-4k, \\\\ -6+5k=2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nk-10=-4k\\,⟹\\,k=2.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $k=2$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6+5k & =2k \\\\ -6+5(2) & =2(2) \\\\ 4 & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=2.$" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371224 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371225 13194139535866 true] [17592186371224 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371226 13194139535866 true] [17592186371224 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371227 13194139535866 true] [17592186371224 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371228 13194139535866 true] [17592186371224 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371229 13194139535866 true] [17592186371224 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535866 true] [17592186371224 1041 "selection" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371224 1104 #edb/ref 17592186371225 13194139535866 true] [17592186371224 1110 #uuid "18b3c739-5ae2-5776-9cf2-6747ea639499" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371225 1034 "2" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371225 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535866 true] [17592186371225 1111 #uuid "664946aa-cb50-5385-b252-05759f631373" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371226 1034 "1" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371226 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535866 true] [17592186371226 1111 #uuid "0fbb919a-74de-5e76-a351-93a9af2a2c75" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371227 1034 "4" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371227 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535866 true] [17592186371227 1111 #uuid "24f55770-03f9-5dcf-aefd-1ce53ffa437d" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371228 1034 "0" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371228 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535866 true] [17592186371228 1111 #uuid "3821c5d3-7e4f-5757-9b80-a411995b54ec" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371229 1034 "-1" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371229 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535866 true] [17592186371229 1111 #uuid "9032fa7a-07e5-5773-8f28-b6e3b6a3c228" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371230 1017 #edb/ref 17592186371231 13194139535866 true] [17592186371230 1089 "q-108044" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371230 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535866 true] [17592186371230 1100 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2,$ then $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-6.$" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371230 1113 #uuid "d6270985-6d2a-5318-afb3-6b0266d6b180" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371230 1145 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1$ (but not $-1$).\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=-3v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(3v)=3Av=3(-2v)=-2(3v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(3v)=-2(3v),$ and $3v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-2$ (but not $-6$).\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139535866 true] [17592186371231 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371232 13194139535866 true] [17592186371231 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371233 13194139535866 true] [17592186371231 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371234 13194139535866 true] [17592186371231 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371235 13194139535866 true] [17592186371231 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371236 13194139535866 true] [17592186371231 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535866 true] [17592186371231 1041 "selection" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371231 1104 #edb/ref 17592186371236 13194139535866 true] [17592186371231 1110 #uuid "fd2bf80e-f9fd-5288-89f6-16356c5fb6fd" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371232 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371232 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535866 true] [17592186371232 1111 #uuid "ae9baf22-6b0f-5a88-848f-e8fb9c700e1a" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371233 1034 "I and III only" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371233 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535866 true] [17592186371233 1111 #uuid "016fd12e-792a-527b-9b0d-edbc565722ef" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371234 1034 "I only" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371234 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535866 true] [17592186371234 111