 \\\\  & =-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=-3v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3.$" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371245 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371246 13194139535866 true] [17592186371245 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371247 13194139535866 true] [17592186371245 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371248 13194139535866 true] [17592186371245 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371249 13194139535866 true] [17592186371245 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371250 13194139535866 true] [17592186371245 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535866 true] [17592186371245 1041 "selection" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371245 1104 #edb/ref 17592186371249 13194139535866 true] [17592186371245 1110 #uuid "e41c7791-1467-51fd-884b-2b3217727656" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371246 1034 "-5" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371246 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535866 true] [17592186371246 1111 #uuid "d3d80542-5bc8-50ba-a3be-ab72d049d270" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371247 1034 "3" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371247 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535866 true] [17592186371247 1111 #uuid "922bc24e-b624-53c9-9356-b7de8d37028c" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371248 1034 "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371248 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535866 true] [17592186371248 1111 #uuid "0681ef85-b26d-5a02-b7e6-8fd3df2c0fdb" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371249 1034 "-3" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371249 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535866 true] [17592186371249 1111 #uuid "c401fd4b-37ae-5d65-bcd1-5ad3f89d6937" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371250 1034 "5" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371250 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535866 true] [17592186371250 1111 #uuid "7c267445-7910-5c74-a794-f2decac63c94" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371251 1017 #edb/ref 17592186371252 13194139535866 true] [17592186371251 1089 "q-75611" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371251 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535866 true] [17592186371251 1100 "The eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 20-4k & 3\\end{aligned}]$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2k+1$ is  $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Determine the value of $k.$" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371251 1113 #uuid "1c4e0639-18e2-5768-9e5f-81dd26992565" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371251 1145 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\lambda v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 20-4k & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =(2k+1)[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 26-4k\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2k+1 \\\\ 4k+2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}7=2k+1, \\\\ 26-4k=4k+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $k$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n7=2k+1\\,⟹\\,k=3.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $k=3$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}26-4k & =4k+2 \\\\ 26-4\\cdot 3 & =4\\cdot 3+2 \\\\ 14 & =14,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a true statement.\n\nTherefore, the solution of the system is $k=3.$" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371252 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371253 13194139535866 true] [17592186371252 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371254 13194139535866 true] [17592186371252 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371255 13194139535866 true] [17592186371252 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371256 13194139535866 true] [17592186371252 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371257 13194139535866 true] [17592186371252 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535866 true] [17592186371252 1041 "selection" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371252 1104 #edb/ref 17592186371253 13194139535866 true] [17592186371252 1110 #uuid "31c46150-14a8-5d7e-81f8-99a46e83ab9b" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371253 1034 "3" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371253 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535866 true] [17592186371253 1111 #uuid "8149b2d9-d323-521f-903c-42b115ae4c68" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371254 1034 "7" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371254 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535866 true] [17592186371254 1111 #uuid "985df5db-1f11-56a4-a917-a84b1394da5d" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371255 1034 "2" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371255 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535866 true] [17592186371255 1111 #uuid "ee9401c3-c4c7-5492-a6c0-3ec82367ba91" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371256 1034 "-3" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371256 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535866 true] [17592186371256 1111 #uuid "684e2788-0f2c-572b-8fd5-0410e5364d04" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371257 1034 "-2" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371257 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535866 true] [17592186371257 1111 #uuid "5097ad64-b06c-5d1f-9671-c6a62c58096b" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371258 1017 #edb/ref 17592186371259 13194139535866 true] [17592186371258 1089 "q-75729" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371258 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535866 true] [17592186371258 1100 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =-2.$\n- An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the same eigenvalue $\\lambda =1.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then $7v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371258 1113 #uuid "d7a91e6f-ec17-5b19-a508-d46535ff5b9a" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371258 1145 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement is false. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=2v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=2{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $2$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=\\frac{1}{2}v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{1}=1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(7v)=7Av=7(2v)=2(7v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(7v)=2(7v),$ and $7v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $7v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371259 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371260 13194139535866 true] [17592186371259 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371261 13194139535866 true] [17592186371259 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371262 13194139535866 true] [17592186371259 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371263 13194139535866 true] [17592186371259 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371264 13194139535866 true] [17592186371259 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535866 true] [17592186371259 1041 "selection" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371259 1104 #edb/ref 17592186371264 13194139535866 true] [17592186371259 1110 #uuid "ae1e310c-86ec-50a8-97d0-bf0935117919" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371260 1034 "I only" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371260 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535866 true] [17592186371260 1111 #uuid "88d3d43b-7e5e-5960-8512-650afbd429ec" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371261 1034 "III only" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371261 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535866 true] [17592186371261 1111 #uuid "b670d7bf-02b1-5311-89ce-c85f33a09e8a" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371262 1034 "None" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371262 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535866 true] [17592186371262 1111 #uuid "5f0aee3a-36e5-5b4c-86d9-7245db95edbc" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371263 1034 "I and II only" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371263 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535866 true] [17592186371263 1111 #uuid "3b0b9e07-f029-5bdf-8a52-3f5fa8798cb2" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371264 1034 "II only" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371264 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535866 true] [17592186371264 1111 #uuid "2721dd86-c2ae-50f7-8282-f7e7cb0c5394" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371265 1017 #edb/ref 17592186371266 13194139535866 true] [17592186371265 1089 "q-75730" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371265 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535866 true] [17592186371265 1100 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}$ then $v$ is also an eigenvector of the matrix ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =4.$\n- An eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is also an eigenvector of the inverse ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the same eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5,$ then $-v$ is also an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =5.$" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371265 1113 #uuid "a582afda-86b0-5457-9d06-6c4604568827" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371265 1145 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. By the definition of an eigenvector, we have \n\n$$\nAv=\\frac{1}{4}v.\n$$\n\n \nMultiplying it by ${A}^{-1}$ from the left, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{4}{A}^{-1}v.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $4$, we get \n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}v=4v.\n$$\n\n \nSo, $v$ is an eigenvector  of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $4.$\n\n**Note:** In general, an eigenvector of an invertible matrix $A$ that corresponds to an eigenvalue $\\lambda $ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\frac{1}{\\lambda }.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the statement above, an eigenvector of $A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvector of ${A}^{-1}$ that corresponds to $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{-1}=-1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(-v)=-Av=-(5v)=5(-v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(-v)=5(-v),$ and $-v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $-v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"." 13194139535866 true] [17592186371266 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371267 13194139535866 true] [17592186371266 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371268 13194139535866 true] [17592186371266 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371269 13194139535866 true] [17592186371266 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371270 13194139535866 true] [17592186371266 1000 #edb/ref 17592186371271 13194139535866 true] [17592186371266 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535866 true] [17592186371266 1041 "selection" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371266 1104 #edb/ref 17592186371267 13194139535866 true] [17592186371266 1110 #uuid "f96961ad-100f-5975-97ce-574a82ece562" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371267 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371267 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535866 true] [17592186371267 1111 #uuid "e99e7e7f-1ade-5fba-80f5-ca53e99d12aa" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371268 1034 "II and III only" 13194139535866 true] [17592186371268